m weights.\n   - For each edge (u, v, w):\n        cu = refinement[u], cv = refinement[v]\n        if cu == cv: key = (cu, cv)\n        else: key = tuple(sorted([cu, cv]))\n        total[key] += w\n   - Convert to list: for (c1, c2), w in total.items(): [c1, c2, w]\n   - Sort list by (c1, c2).\n4. Compute members:\n   - Use defaultdict(list)\n   - For each vertex, community in refinement.items(): members[community].append(vertex)\n   - For each community, sort the list.\n   - Convert to dict (order doesn't matter). Optionally sort keys for output.\n5. Return {\"aggregate_edges\": agg_edges, \"members\": members_dict}\n\nCheck that all keys are present; no extra.\n\nNow produce the final response: a JSON with single key \"implementation\" containing the Python source as a string, properly escaped.\n\nWrite the code carefully, ensuring no external dependencies, no mutation of case.\n\nWe'll write:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    edges = case['edges']\n    refinement = case['refinement']\n    from collections import defaultdict\n    agg = defaultdict(float)\n    for u, v, w in edges:\n        cu = refinement[u]\n        cv = refinement[v]\n        if cu == cv:\n            key = (cu, cv)\n        else:\n            key = tuple(sorted([cu, cv]))\n        agg[key] += w\n    aggregate_edges = sorted([[c1, c2, w] for (c1, c2), w in agg.items()])\n    members = defaultdict(list)\n    for v, c in refinement.items():\n        members[c].append(v)\n    # sort each list\n    for c in members:\n        members[c].sort()\n    # sort keys for output (optional)\n    members_dict = {c: members[c] for c in sorted(members.keys())}\n    return {\"aggregate_edges\": aggregate_edges, \"members\": members_dict}\n\nBut note: the schema says \"aggregate_edges\" items must be array with exactly 3 elements (prefixItems). Our list of [c1, c2, w] is fine. w is number, may be int or float; we keep as float? The weights are ints, sum is int, but JSON number is fine.\n\nOne nuance: The schema requires \"